      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we need to check if each function is one-to-one (injective). A function is one-to-one if it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that no horizontal line intersects the graph of the function more than once. If a function fails the horizontal line test, it is not one-to-one (and thus not invertible).\n\nLet's analyze each function:\n\n1. **Graph A**: The graph is a cubic polynomial shifted vertically and has a local maximum. Since the function is not strictly increasing or decreasing between the local maximum and the end points, it fails the horizontal line test. Therefore, Graph A does not have an inverse.\n\n2. **Graph B**: The graph is a parabola opening upwards with a vertex at the origin. Since the parabola is entirely on one side of the line \\(y = x\\), it fails the horizontal line test. Therefore, Graph B does not have an inverse.\n\n3. **Graph C**: The graph is a quarter of a circle with radius 4, centered at the origin, and symmetric about the y-axis. This function is one-to-one because it is strictly increasing on the interval \\([-2, 2]\\) and strictly decreasing on the intervals \\([-4, -2)\\) and \\((2, 4]\\). Therefore, Graph C has an inverse.\n\n4. **Graph D**: The graph is a line with the equation \\(y = 2x\\). This function is one-to-one because for any \\(x\\), there is exactly one \\(y\\) and for any \\(y\\), there is exactly one \\(x\\). Therefore, Graph D has an inverse.\n\n5. **Graph E**: The graph is a cubic polynomial with a positive leading coefficient, indicating it has a local maximum at some point. Since the function is not one-to-one (it repeats values for some \\(x\\)), it fails the horizontal line test. Therefore, Graph E does not have an inverse.\n\nThe functions that have inverses are Graphs C and D. Therefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{C, D}\\).",
